Dr. Darth Vader asked:

>
>1. Is it worth doing Sokoban?  I could always bless magic lamp and
>wish up SDSM, and then I'd have reflection and a BoH.

I think it is always worth doing Sokoban for the experience, plus you always
find some useful items.  It is always good to stockpile some sources for wishes
in case something goes wrong later.  Once or twice I found I sorely needed a
wish just before the endgame.  E.g., I like to find out if Ft. Ludios exists in
my game before I burn wishes on some armour items.

>2. Is it worth entering Sokoban and reverse-genociding nurses?  ie -
>do mid-game wizards have hitpoint issues?

Yes, as to the HP issues, but then again I ran into some nasty air elementals. 
I have never nurse-danced in Sokoban though.  I usually wait for the castle.


>3. How close am I to being ready for the quest?  And what am I to
>expect?  Any tips?

The W quest is not particularly difficult, as the others said.  You do want to
have a Bl BOH, because you will find a lot of items.

>4. Do wizards normally do the castle or the quest first?  (Medusa
>won't be a problem - I have a pickaxe, a towel, and a mirror)

I have done the quest first, but I have only ascended 2 Ws, and none in 3.4. 
(I had one die in Moloch's tomb level and one on the final level of the astral
plane).  It depends on your game.  You might need to get more experience before
the quest or want to look for more altars.

>5. Are there any glaring gaps in my equipment?

I usually end up with far more equipment than I can ever use, and I often
change my gear.  Before tackling Gehennom, I store it all the at the castle. 
You do need a +FoD and need to practice the spell.  Increasing your magical
energy is also important.

Never forget the power of the rubber chicken!

A kick-ass pet, like an A, who can wield artefact weapons that you cannot, can
help a great deal late in the game.  If you can get yourself a pet dragon early
on, they can be annoying in some ways, but also very helpful. 
